= Milk (Cow's Milk)

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Lac_ (genitive _lactis_), neuter. Cow's milk specifically _lac vaccinum_ or _lac bovinum_. Root in PIE \*_glakt-_ / \*_melg-_ ("to milk, stroke off"). _Lac_ is cognate with Greek _γάλα_ (_gála_, gen. _γάλακτος gálaktos_) — source of "galactose," "lactose," "lactation." The \*_melg-_ root gives Latin _mulgēre_ ("to milk") and the Germanic words.#footnote[Etymologies: Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_ (s.v. _lac_); de Vaan, _Etymological Dictionary of Latin and the Other Italic Languages_; for the PIE \*_melg-_ / \*_glakt-_ roots, Watkins, _The American Heritage Dictionary of Indo-European Roots_.]

*English:* _Milk_, from Old English _meolc / mioluc_, from Proto-Germanic \*_meluks_, from PIE \*_melg-_. Same root gives the verb "to milk."#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "milk, n.¹"); Watkins, _Indo-European Roots_ (\*_melg-_).]

*Chinese:* 牛奶 (niúnǎi), lit. "cow-milk" (牛 niú = ox/cow; 奶 nǎi = milk/breast). Classical materia medica register: 牛乳 (niúrǔ), where 乳 (rǔ) is the literary/classical word for milk and the term used in the _Bencao_ literature; 乳 originally depicts a mother nursing. Classical entries are headed 牛乳.#footnote[Character glosses: _Hanyu Da Cidian_ (漢語大詞典); the 牛乳 entry heading follows Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578, animal-products section.]

*Sanskrit:* दुग्ध (_dugdha_), "that which has been milked" (past passive participle of √_duh_, "to milk, draw out, yield"). The other common term is क्षीर (_kṣīra_). Cow's milk specifically गोक्षीर (_go-kṣīra_) or धेनुदुग्ध; गो (_go_) = cow.#footnote[Monier-Williams, _A Sanskrit–English Dictionary_ (s.v. _dugdha_, _kṣīra_, _go_); root √_duh_ per Whitney, _The Roots, Verb-Forms and Primary Derivatives of the Sanskrit Language_.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Slightly cold to neutral (微寒 / 平).#footnote[Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_ (本草綱目), 1578, 牛乳 entry; earliest property attributions in _Mingyi Bielu_ (名醫別錄) and Sun Simiao, _Qianjin Shizhi_ (千金食治). English: Unschuld, _Ben Cao Gang Mu_ (Univ. of California Press, 2021).]

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Sweet (甘 gān).

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Heart (心), Lung (肺), Stomach (胃); some sources add Spleen.#footnote[Systematized channel/property data: Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (Eastland Press, 2004).]

*Actions & indications:*
- 補虛羸 — tonifies vacuity and emaciation/weakness.
- 益肺胃 — benefits Lung and Stomach.
- 養血、生津、潤燥 — nourishes Blood, generates fluids (jin), moistens dryness.
- 潤腸 — moistens intestines; used for dryness constipation and 消渴 (xiaoke, wasting-thirst).
- Indicated for 反胃 (counterflow/regurgitation) and dry retching.#footnote[Action list compiled from the _Bencao Gangmu_ 牛乳 entry and standard modern TCM dietetics; cf. Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.]

*Cautions:* Sweet, moistening, slightly cold — cloying, can generate dampness/phlegm (生濕, 滑腸); unsuitable for Spleen vacuity with damp, loose stools, or phlegm-damp. Sun Simiao warned against taking it cold or carelessly combining with sour foods.#footnote[Sun Simiao, _Qianjin Yaofang / Shizhi_ (千金食治); dietary cautions also in the _Bencao Gangmu_ 牛乳 entry.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

Cow's milk (गोक्षीर _go-kṣīra_) is the paradigm and most praised of the eight classical milks.#footnote[The eight milks (_aṣṭau kṣīrāṇi_) and go-kṣīra's pre-eminence: Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27 (_annapānavidhi_, milk section); Suśruta Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 45 (_dravadravya-vidhi_).]

*Rasa (taste):* Madhura (मधुर, sweet).

*Guṇa (qualities):* Guru (गुरु, heavy), Snigdha (स्निग्ध, unctuous); also Manda, Mṛdu, Bahala, Picchila, Sāra. Compact descriptor: _guru-snigdha_.

*Vīrya (potency):* Śīta (शीत, cooling).

*Vipāka (post-digestive taste):* Madhura (sweet).#footnote[Rasa–guṇa–vīrya–vipāka of go-kṣīra: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Dugdha-varga_ (Chunekar & Pandey ed., Chaukhamba); Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27.]

*Prabhāva (specific action):* No distinct prabhāva assigned to plain go-kṣīra in the classical texts — its actions flow from sweet rasa, cooling vīrya, sweet vipāka, and heavy-unctuous guṇa. Its _ojas-vardhana_ / _jīvanīya_ capacity and near-universal _sātmya_ are sometimes treated as prabhāva-level.#footnote[On prabhāva as a category (action not explicable from rasa–vīrya–vipāka): Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 26.67–72. The "prabhāva-level" reading of milk's ojas-building action is interpretive (commentarial / modern dravyaguna), not a stated classical prabhāva — flagged as such.]

*Doṣa effect:* Pacifies Vāta and Pitta (वातपित्तहर); increases Kapha.#footnote[Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27; Aṣṭāṅga Hṛdaya, Sūtrasthāna 5 (Murthy translation, Chaukhamba).]

*Karma (actions) — Caraka's classic go-kṣīra list:*
- _Jīvanīya_ — vitalizing, life-sustaining.
- _Rasāyana_ — rejuvenative.
- _Balya / Bṛṃhaṇa_ — strengthening, nourishing/anabolic.
- _Vṛṣya_ — aphrodisiac, promotes _śukra_.
- _Medhya_ — promotes intellect.
- _Stanya_ (_stanyajanana_) — promotes lactation.
- _Śramahara, Klamahara_ — relieves fatigue and exhaustion.
- _Śvāsa-kāsahara_ — relieves dyspnea and cough.
- _Raktapittahara_ — pacifies bleeding/Pitta-blood disorders.
- _Pathya, nitya-sevanīya_ — wholesome, suitable for daily use.#footnote[The karma list closely follows Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27.217–224 (go-kṣīra verses); cf. Suśruta Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 45. Translations: Sharma & Dash, _Caraka Saṃhitā_ (Chowkhamba).]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional)_

- *(S) Fish (matsya)* — the locus classicus of _viruddha-āhāra_, said to cause _rakta-duṣṭi_ and _kuṣṭha_; especially _cilcima_ fish.
- *(S) Sour substances (amla)*; milk curdles and obstructs the channels (_srotas_).
- *(S) Kapittha* (wood-apple, _Limonia acidissima_) — explicitly named.
- *(S) Salt (lavaṇa)*.
- *(S) Horse gram (kulattha, _Macrotyloma uniflorum_)* and sour/heating pulses.
- *(S) Other flesh/meat (māṃsa)*.
- *(S) Sesame / sesame paste (tila)*.
- *(S) Green leafy vegetables (certain śāka)*.
- *(S) Radish (mūlaka)* and pungent items.
- *(N) Banana (kadalī)* with milk and buttermilk.
- *(N) Jackfruit and various sour/heating fruits*.
- *(N) Black gram (māṣa)* in some preparations.#footnote[Core milk incompatibilities (fish, sour, kapittha, kulattha, etc.): Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 26.81–101 (_viruddhāhāra_); Suśruta Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 20 (_hitāhitīya_). Banana, jackfruit, and several fruit pairings derive from later nighaṇṭu and regional literature rather than the core saṃhitās — lower source tier, flagged (N).]

*Other viruddha categories:*
- *Saṃskāra viruddha:* heated/curdled milk with incompatible processing.
- *Mātrā / krama viruddha:* wrong quantity or sequence.
- *Conditional (doṣa, satmya):* inappropriate in Kapha excess, _āma_ states, weak _agni_, abdominal distension, certain skin diseases.#footnote[The eighteen categories of viruddha (including _saṃskāra_, _mātrā_, _krama_, _satmya_): Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 26.86–101.]

#emph[Caveat:] Sources don't fully agree; some entries (banana, jackfruit) are later/regional. No robust modern toxicological basis confirms the milk-fish or milk-fruit combinations as harmful; they remain classical injunctions.

*Notes on form:* _Dhārā-uṣṇa_ (fresh warm) milk is lightest and tridoṣa-balancing; boiled-reduced heavier; cold uncooked heaviest and hardest to digest.#footnote[Effect of form/processing on milk's qualities: Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27; Aṣṭāṅga Hṛdaya, Sūtrasthāna 5.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Milk is one of the most-praised foods in Tibb — the canonical complete nourishment of the Greco-Arabic tradition. See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ _for the foundational framework._

*Arabic:* لبن (_laban_) — generic milk; حليب (_ḥalīb_) — fresh sweet milk; cow's milk specifically لبن البقر (_laban al-baqar_). Persian: شیر (_shīr_). Urdu/Hakeemi: دودھ (_dūdh_).

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation:* Qur'an 16:66 (Sūrat al-Naḥl): "We give you to drink of what is in their bellies — between excretion and blood — *pure milk, palatable to the drinkers*"; Qur'an 47:15 (rivers of milk among the paradisal drinks). Multiple hadith on the Prophet's drinking of milk and his special blessing on it (Bukhari, Muslim, _Kitāb al-Ashriba_); the recommended *milk-as-perfect-food* register is foundational. The Prophet's preference for *camel milk* in some contexts is documented (Bukhari, _Kitāb al-Ṭibb_, the hadith of the people of ʿUrayna who were cured by camel milk + urine — the controversial pharmacological reading lies outside modern medical opinion).

*Mizāj:* *Cold and Moist in the 1st degree* (Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _laban_ entry; Ibn al-Bayṭār, *al-Jāmiʿ*). The classification converges with the Ayurvedic _śīta vīrya_ (cooling) — a clean cross-system convergence on the same substance.

*Af'āl:* *Muqawwī al-badan* (body-strengthening); *muraṭṭib* (humectant / moistening); *mughadhdhī* (nourishing); *muḥsin li-l-bashara* (skin-improving); *mufariḥ* (mood-elevating); *muqawwī al-rūḥ al-ḥaywānī* (vital-spirit tonic, modest). Camel milk specifically: *mufattiḥ* (deobstruent) and *muqawwī al-kabid* (hepatic tonic); the Tibb register holds camel milk as more medicinal than cow milk, while cow milk is the better general nourishment.

*Istemālāt:* *amrāḍ al-sawdā'* (melancholic / dryness diseases — the principal indication); chronic cough with dryness; debility; convalescence; pregnancy and lactation support; insomnia (warm milk before sleep is the canonical Tibb prescription). Camel milk specifically for *amrāḍ al-kabid* (hepatic diseases) and *amrāḍ al-juld* (skin diseases including chronic psoriasis-like conditions).

*Mawāne':* phlegmatic constitution (*mizāj balghamī*) in excess; lactose intolerance (modern); colic of small children (in some Tibb readings); weak gastric fire.

*Muṣliḥ:* *honey* — the classical pairing for warming and digesting cool moist milk; *cardamom* and *saffron* for aromatic enhancement; *ginger* for the phlegmatic constitution.

*Badal:* almond milk (*lubāb al-lawz*); the classical Unani almond-milk preparation (*sharbat-i lawz*) is the standard substitute when fresh milk is unavailable or contraindicated.

*Tibb formulas:* *Khamīrah-i Marwārīd* (pearl-electuary with milk base); *Sharbat-i Bahār* with milk; almond-milk preparations; *Falūdah / Phālūdah* — Persian milk-and-noodle dessert with medicinal status; *qishr* and *khoa* / *māwa* dairy reductions used as bases for Mughal Unani sweets.

*Source attestation:* Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _laban_ entry — the foundational treatment, with sub-entries for cow / sheep / goat / camel / mare milks; Ibn al-Bayṭār, *al-Jāmiʿ*, _laban_ and species-specific entries; *Khazāyin al-Adwiya*, vol. III, _dūdh_ entry — modern Urdu standard; Ibn Qayyim al-Jawziyya, _al-Ṭibb al-Nabawī_, milk chapter.#footnote[Tibb laban: Avicenna, _Qānūn_ II; Ibn al-Bayṭār; Khazāyin al-Adwiya. Camel milk in Prophetic medicine: Bukhari, _Kitāb al-Ṭibb_, hadith of ʿUrayna. The cross-system convergence with Ayurvedic _kṣīra_ is exact.]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Approximate composition, whole cow's milk, per 100 g (≈100 mL). *Verify against USDA FoodData Central*, current edition; values vary by fat content, breed, fortification:#footnote[Composition figures are approximate and should be checked against USDA FoodData Central (https://fdc.nal.usda.gov), current edition, as values vary by fat content, breed, and fortification.]

- Energy ~60–65 kcal; Water ~88 g
- Protein ~3.2–3.4 g (≈80% casein, ≈20% whey — β-lactoglobulin, α-lactalbumin)
- Fat ~3.2–3.5 g (whole); short/medium-chain and saturated fatty acids, plus CLA
- Carbohydrate ~4.8 g, essentially all *lactose* (glucose + galactose)
- Calcium ~110–120 mg (bioavailability aided by casein phosphopeptides and lactose)
- Phosphorus ~90 mg; Potassium ~140–150 mg
- Vitamin B12 ~0.4–0.5 µg; Riboflavin (B2) ~0.18 mg (both notable)
- Vitamin A and D in the fat; D meaningful mainly when fortified
- Iodine — significant in many regions

*Pharmacology / physiology of note:*
- *Lactose* needs intestinal lactase (β-galactosidase). Lactase non-persistence (ancestral for most humans) underlies adult lactose intolerance — a biochemical correlate of the cloying/damp/hard-to-digest cautions.#footnote[On lactase persistence/non-persistence and adult-type hypolactasia: standard reviews in human genetics and gastroenterology — verify against current literature (e.g. reviews in _The American Journal of Clinical Nutrition_).]
- *Casein* clots under stomach acid/rennin and digests slowly (a "slow" protein) — echoing the _guru_ classification. Acid-curdling is also the literal mechanism behind the _amla_ (sour) viruddha.
- *Bioactive peptides* from digestion include casomorphins (opioid-like) and ACE-inhibitory peptides.#footnote[Milk-derived bioactive peptides (casomorphins, ACE-inhibitory peptides): dairy-science reviews — verify against current literature.]
- *Tryptophan + calcium + casomorphins* give a plausible mechanism for milk's cross-traditional calming/sleep reputation.
- *Milk-fish incompatibility:* no robust modern toxicological basis established; remains a classical injunction.

*Safe daily intake:* No toxicological upper limit in the medicinal sense — milk is a whole food. Practical considerations rather than a toxic ceiling: individual lactose tolerance, saturated-fat intake within overall dietary limits, and general guidance of roughly 2–3 dairy servings/day in many national guidelines (not a safety threshold).#footnote[Dairy-serving guidance varies by country and is dietary-pattern advice, not a safety limit; verify against current national dietary guidelines (e.g. USDA Dietary Guidelines for Americans, or the Brazilian _Guia Alimentar para a População Brasileira_).]

